  "prediction": "The Office of Personnel Management (OPM) reports that there are approximately 2.1 million civilian workers in the federal workforce, but the total can vary depending on the methodology and data source used. OPM and the Office of Management and Budget (OMB) compile and publish statistics on the federal workforce, with differences in their totals due to varying methods and exclusions.\n\nOne key distinction is between \"full-time equivalents\" (FTEs) and on-board personnel. FTEs are calculated based on the total number of regular hours worked by employees, while on-board personnel refers to the number of employees in pay status at a given time. Table 1 in the report provides examples of how these methods can result in different federal workforce totals.\n\nOMB uses FTE employment numbers to manage employment in departments and agencies and requires reporting in the President's Budget. OPM defines on-board employment as the number of employees in pay status at the end of a quarter and provides headcounts through its Employment and Trends report and FedScope database.\n\nThe EHRI-SDM, established by OPM in FY2010, is the most comprehensive resource on the size and scope of the federal workforce, covering 96% of nonpostal federal civilian executive branch employees. However, it has exclusions, including some national security and intelligence agencies and the Postal Service.\n\nFedScope is a public website that provides access to employment, accession, and separation data for approximately 120 federal agencies. It offers data in five subject categories, including Employment, Accession, Separation, Employment Trends, and Diversity.\n\nThe report also mentions other resources, such as Employment and Trends, Common Characteristics of Government, and Sizing Up the Executive Branch of the Federal Workforce, which provide various data on the federal workforce. Users should be aware of the differences in methodologies and exclusions when using federal workforce statistics from these sources.",
  "scored_prediction": "The Office of Personnel Management (OPM) reports that there are approximately 2.1 million civilian workers in the federal workforce, but the total can vary depending on the methodology and data source used. OPM and the Office of Management and Budget (OMB) compile and publish statistics on the federal workforce, with differences in their totals due to varying methods and exclusions.\n\nOne key distinction is between \"full-time equivalents\" (FTEs) and on-board personnel. FTEs are calculated based on the total number of regular hours worked by employees, while on-board personnel refers to the number of employees in pay status at a given time. Table 1 in the report provides examples of how these methods can result in different federal workforce totals.\n\nOMB uses FTE employment numbers to manage employment in departments and agencies and requires reporting in the President's Budget. OPM defines on-board employment as the number of employees in pay status at the end of a quarter and provides headcounts through its Employment and Trends report and FedScope database.\n\nThe EHRI-SDM, established by OPM in FY2010, is the most comprehensive resource on the size and scope of the federal workforce, covering 96% of nonpostal federal civilian executive branch employees. However, it has exclusions, including some national security and intelligence agencies and the Postal Service.\n\nFedScope is a public website that provides access to employment, accession, and separation data for approximately 120 federal agencies. It offers data in five subject categories, including Employment, Accession, Separation, Employment Trends, and Diversity.\n\nThe report also mentions other resources, such as Employment and Trends, Common Characteristics of Government, and Sizing Up the Executive Branch of the Federal Workforce, which provide various data on the federal workforce. Users should be aware of the differences in methodologies and exclusions when using federal workforce statistics from these sources.",
  "score": 23.43096194331332,
  "answers": [
    "This report describes online tools, reports, and data compilations created by the Office of Management and Budget (OMB) and the Office of Personnel Management (OPM) that contain statistics about federal employees and the federal workforce. The report also describes key characteristics of each resource and briefly discusses selected methodological differences, with the intention of facilitating the selection of appropriate data for specific purposes. This report is not intended to be a definitive list of all information on the federal workforce. It describes significant and recurring products that contain specific data often requested by Members or congressional staff."
  ],
